{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And Aaron lifted up his hands toward the people and blessed them.</b> The sense of אל העם is: for the need of the people and for their benefit. And some say here that Aaron would lift up his hand and gesture with it toward the people this way and that, just as they are accustomed in their nation with a motion of their right hand; and they brought proof from the statement of the Rabbis of blessed memory: ״ידיו״ — ״ידו״ is written. And our reply to them, to stop the mouth of the speaker of arrogance, is that ידיו is read, and both hands he would lift up; so their claim collapses, for it concerns the motion of one hand, namely the right. And what the Rabbis of blessed memory said, ״ידו is written,״ is because the right is more honored than the left in the matter of the hidden, which is revealed among the individuals (יחידים); therefore the recipients of truth said ״ידו is written,״ and then the verse and the Masorah ״ידיו / ידו״ are both fulfilled. And so you find in the lifting of hands that Moses performed, where Scripture mentions there ״ידיו״ and ״ידו״: (Exodus 17:11) ״והיה כאשר ירים משה ידו,״ and ״והיו ידיו אמונה״ — and it did not mention ״heaven,״ because there is no need, since it is well known that the lifting was toward heaven, and the same applies to the expression וישא. And if it were as the disputants claim, that Aaron would spread out his hands toward the people and bless them, it would have been fitting to say ״וישלח אהרן את ידיו אל העם,״ for so is the way of a father when he wishes to bless his son, to place his hand upon his head, in the manner that Jacob did to Joseph, of which it is written (Genesis 48:14) ״וישלח ישראל את ימינו״; but it says וישא to indicate that his hands were raised upright upward, in the manner of the verse spoken of Solomon (I Kings 8:54): ״ויהי ככלות שלמה להתפלל אל ה׳ את כל התפלה והתחנה הזאת וכפיו פרושות השמימה,״ and ״ויעמוד ויברך את כל קהל ישראל״ — Scripture explained that his hands were raised upright upward, and he blessed Israel."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {0 "The phrase נגלה אצל היחידים is translated hyper-literally; the reference is to a kabbalic distinction between the hidden (סתר) and that revealed among singular/unique entities. The end of the I Kings quotation reads וכפיו פרושות השמימה in the Hebrew here (with definite article), matching the verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:26.495378472Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}